## Error handler does not ordinarily return

0x6cd8's normal control flow may call recovery0x63b8 at0x6e2c. Its terminal
fallthrough performs cleanup and at0x6e90 executes BL0x1fc (bytesd9e4ffeb).
0x1fc clearsr0-r12;0x230 contains EAFFFFFE, an unconditional self-loop.
The apparent POP/BX-LR epilogue at0x6e94/0x6e98 is therefore not reached by an
ordinary returning path. Neither BL nor a visible epilogue proves a soft check.
This does not assert a complete anti-rollback policy for every boot path.

The separate logger0x6bec has a genuine returning path at0x6ca8/0x6cac.
Its default implementation appends24-byte records until count reaches20,
then returns0 without appending; it does not wrap in the inspected path.
A registered hook can replace that default path, and invalid input enters
0x6cd8. The blanket label 'log-and-return' belongs neither to all error
handling nor to the0x6cd8 routine.

## SRAM ownership is phase-specific

0x6d60 loads literal0x20000;0x6d64..0x6d74 writes the log header including
capacity0x14. This is compatible with a separately acquired RPM MBN image
whose header declares load address0x20000. That RPM image's SHA256 is
157ac0122f3158b298cf735ed7a001cdde1b98d3d635ae33b472c0d4efec58e8, recorded in
samcday/pocketboot refs/notes/evidence on4b7685eac406948aed645f2f4377649af3b3b28e.
The header declaration is not an independent trace of its later execution.
The clear from0x201e8 through0x2c000 at0x6e74 is error cleanup, not proof of
an undifferentiated normal-startup .data/.bss region.

## The expected comparison reference is caller-supplied

0x60b0: LDR r3,[r4,#4], where r4 is authentication contextA.
0x60b8: BL0xf840.

0xf840 PUSHes13 registers (r0-r11,lr), subtracting0x34 bytes; incomingr3
is then at SP+0x0c.0xf844 subtracts another0x44 bytes. Relative to this new
SP, saved incomingr3 is at SP+0x50, NOT SP+0x0c.
If entrySP=E: savedr3=E-0x28; currentSP=E-0x78; currentSP+0x50=E-0x28.
Explicit SP+0x50 accesses in f840..fbd8 are loads atf894,faac,fb58. The
frame unwinds only atfbb8 (ADDsp,#0x54, then POP9 registers).

0xfb40: ADD r3,sp,#0x0c supplies the local computed-output buffer.
0xfb48: MOV r0,#2;0xfb4c: BL0x7fa0.
0xfb58: LDR r1,[sp,#0x50] retrieves the expected-reference pointer.
0xfb5c: MOV r2,#0x20;0xfb60: ADD r0,sp,#0x0c.
0xfb64: BL0x2484 compares32 bytes.
Thus SP+0x0c is computed output and SP+0x50 the saved caller pointer at this
point. Confusing post-PUSH and post-SUB offsets reverses their interpretation.

0x61bc initializes the context.0x61e4 loads r8=0x119a4. One configuration
path reaches0x6254 ADD r0,r8,r4,LSL#5 after an index<13 check;0x6258 stores
the selected pointer atA+4. Another path passes RAM0x20640 to0x869c and
stores that returned pointer.0x869c reads hardware words near0x7042d8/
0x7042dc in six5-byte chunks, then two bytes from0x704308, populating32 bytes.
The first context wordA+0 is the cached auth-enabled flag, not that index.

0xfa94 and0xfaa8 reference constants0x11b68/0x11b7c in additional comparisons;
they do not replace the final comparison against the saved caller pointer.
The incoming assertion that those two constants alone establish a sole
immutable trust anchor is unsupported. This also does NOT establish that the
active root is freely replaceable. No actual fuse/cache value was measured.

